/* ============================================================================
is-prime-check.epher -- primality of a list of small integers

Runs the isprime builtin over a sample list and reads the booleans off
one line. The comments below state the small facts the list shows: 1 is
not prime by convention, 2 is the only even prime, and every other even
number is composite. This script exists to show the builtin, so it does
not reimplement the test.

Source: the definition of a prime number, standard number theory (see
Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers", ch. 1).
isprime is exact for every integer the calculator can hold.

Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

There is no knob: the sample list is the point of the script.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the sample ---------------------------------------------------------------
// A list to try: the for loop (guide 1.30) applies isprime to each entry
// and collects one boolean per entry, and the loop itself prints the list.
for x in {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 9, 15, 21, 29, 97, 100, 101} do print("isprime(", x, "):", isprime(x))

/* ---- what the line shows ------------------------------------------------------
0 is not prime (a prime is a positive integer with exactly two divisors).
1 is not prime by convention: its only divisor is itself, not two.
2 is prime, and it is the only even prime; 3, 5, 7, ... handle the odds.
4, 9, 15, 21 and 100 are composite: 2^2, 3^2, 3 * 5, 3 * 7 and 10^2.
29, 97 and 101 are prime; 97 and 101 are consecutive primes (next script). */
for x in {2, 29, 97, 101} do print("each prime of the sample passes:", x, isprime(x))
/* ---- expected output ----
= {isprime( 0 ): false, isprime( 1 ): false, isprime( 2 ): true, isprime( 3 ): true, isprime( 4 ): false, isprime( 9 ): false, isprime( 15 ): false, isprime( 21 ): false, isprime( 29 ): true, isprime( 97 ): true, isprime( 100 ): false, isprime( 101 ): true}
= {each prime of the sample passes: 2 true, each prime of the sample passes: 29 true, each prime of the sample passes: 97 true, each prime of the sample passes: 101 true}
*/
